            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Slides a pattern along the values and, at each position, measures how well they match:\nthe values under the pattern, each times the pattern's value there, added up. The result is highest where the values look most like the pattern - a template matcher for a gesture, a beat, a shape in a curve.\n\nIt is t.convolve without reversing the pattern, and it is what torch's conv1d does.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.correlate <pattern values ...> <mode>\nThe pattern can be given here: t.correlate 1 2 1. The mode can also be given: same, full\nor valid.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values to search. Every input sends.\n\npattern:\nThe pattern, held.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nThe dim searched along; -1, the last, by default.\n\nmode:\nAn option, under the node: same (the default, the input's length), full, or valid, as for t.convolve.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncorrelated:\nHow well the pattern matches at each position (in same mode, centred on it).\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe pattern 1 2 1 occurs twice in the values, centred at positions 2 and 7: the match is highest, 6, exactly there. t.argmax would find the best one.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.correlate is the numpy version. scipy.correlate finds the delay between two whole signals. t.convolve shapes with a kernel. scipy.find_peaks finds every high point of the match.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1150,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Slides a pattern along the values and, at each position, measures how well they match:\nthe values under the pattern, each times the pattern's value there, added up. The result is highest where the values look most like the pattern - a template matcher for a gesture, a beat, a shape in a curve.\n\nIt is t.convolve without reversing the pattern, and it is what torch's conv1d does.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.correlate <pattern values ...> <mode>\nThe pattern can be given here: t.correlate 1 2 1. The mode can also be given: same, full\nor valid.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values to search. Every input sends.\n\npattern:\nThe pattern, held.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nThe dim searched along; -1, the last, by default.\n\nmode:\nAn option, under the node: same (the default, the input's length), full, or valid, as for t.convolve.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncorrelated:\nHow well the pattern matches at each position (in same mode, centred on it).\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe pattern 1 2 1 occurs twice in the values, centred at positions 2 and 7: the match is highest, 6, exactly there. t.argmax would find the best one.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.correlate is the numpy version. scipy.correlate finds the delay between two whole signals. t.convolve shapes with a kernel. scipy.find_peaks finds every high point of the match."
        },
